## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design streaming data structures with sliding windows, handling batched updates, and rigorous amortized analysis. Also probes correctness under expirations and the practical choice between exact maintenance vs rebuilds.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window frequency tables, heap with lazy deletion, batch amortized analysis, memory locality, worst-case scenarios.

## Code Example
```javascript
class TopFreqWindow {
  constructor(W) { this.W = W; this.cnt = new Map(); this.queue = []; this.heap = new MaxHeap(); }
  batchInsert(vals) {
    for (const v of vals) {
      this.queue.push(v);
      this.cnt.set(v, (this.cnt.get(v) || 0) + 1);
      this.heap.push({ value: v, count: this.cnt.get(v) });
    }
    this.expireIfNeeded();
  }
  expireIfNeeded() {
    while (this.queue.length > this.W) {
      const old = this.queue.shift();
      const c = (this.cnt.get(old) || 0) - 1;
      if (c <= 0) this.cnt.delete(old); else this.cnt.set(old, c);
      this.heap.push({ value: old, count: c });
    }
  }
  top1() {
    while (true) {
      const t = this.heap.peek(); if (!t) return null;
      if ((this.cnt.get(t.value) || 0) === t.count) return t.value;
      this.heap.pop();
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for approximate top-k with space constraints?
- Analyze behavior under adversarial burst patterns and discuss optimizations.

## Why This Is Asked
A practical dynamic 2x2 submatrix counter seizes local updates; analytic challenge: bound work per update and handle edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- Local neighborhood counting; incremental updates
- Amortized O(B) per batch; worst-case O(B) with 4 checks per update
- Space: store full matrix and current count; avoid full scan

## Code Example
```javascript
function updateCell(mat, n, i, j, val, cur) {
  const old = mat[i][j];
  if (old === val) return cur;
  for (let di = -1; di <= 0; di++) {
    for (let dj = -1; dj <= 0; dj++) {
      const r = i + di, c = j + dj;
      if (r >= 0 && c >= 0 && r + 1 < n && c + 1 < n) {
        const before = mat[r][c] & mat[r+1][c] & mat[r][c+1] & mat[r+1][c+1];
        // after flip not yet applied
        // compute new values after applying val to mat[i][j]
        const a = (r === i && c === j) ? val : mat[r][c];
        const b = (r+1 === i && c === j) ? val : mat[r+1][c];
        const c2 = (r === i && c+1 === j) ? val : mat[r][c+1];
        const d = (r+1 === i && c+1 === j) ? val : mat[r+1][c+1];
        const after = a & b & c2 & d;
        if (before !== after) {
          cur += (after ? 1 : -1) * 1; // adjust
        }
      }
    }
  }
  mat[i][j] = val;
  return cur;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to counting 3x3 all-ones squares with O(1) per update? 
- How do you handle non-binary updates or weighted counts?

