## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to design efficient dynamic ranking systems with batched updates, requiring you to balance update locality against query efficiency in real-world scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- Ordered maps for score-based organization
- Lazy maximum score tracking to avoid unnecessary recomputation
- Efficient top-K extraction through bucket traversal
- Amortized time complexity analysis
- Memory usage optimization techniques

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode implementation
class TopKKeeper {
  constructor(users) {
    this.scoreToIds = new TreeMap(); // score -> TreeSet of user IDs
    this.userToScore = new HashMap(); // user ID -> current score
    this.maxScore = null;

    // Initialize in O(n)
    for (const [id, score] of users) {
      this.addUserToBucket(id, score);
    }
  }

  updateBatch(updates) {
    // Process B updates in O(B log n)
    for (const [id, delta] of updates) {
      const oldScore = this.userToScore.get(id);
      const newScore = oldScore + delta;

      this.removeUserFromBucket(id, oldScore);
      this.addUserToBucket(id, newScore);
    }
  }

  getTopK(k) {
    // Extract top-K in O(K log n)
    const result = [];
    let currentScore = this.maxScore;
    let remaining = k;

    while (remaining > 0 && currentScore !== null) {
      const ids = this.scoreToIds.get(currentScore);
      const takeCount = Math.min(remaining, ids.size());

      // Extract first 'takeCount' IDs (already sorted)
      result.push(...Array.from(ids).slice(0, takeCount));
      remaining -= takeCount;
      currentScore = this.scoreToIds.lowerKey(currentScore);
    }

    return result.slice(0, k);
  }
}
```

## Optimizations
1. **Lazy Maximum Updates**: Only recompute the maximum score when the current maximum bucket becomes empty, avoiding O(log n) overhead on every update.

2. **Batch Top-K Extraction**: Cache the current top-K result and only recompute when updates affect users in the top-K, reducing query frequency from every batch to only when necessary.

## Comparison to Rebuilding
Rebuilding from scratch would require O(n) time per batch to sort all users, resulting in O(n × batches) total time. Our approach achieves O(B log n) updates and O(K log n) queries, providing significant improvement when K << n and B is moderate.

## Complexity Analysis
- **Initialization**: O(n) - one insertion per user
- **Batch Updates**: O(B log n) - each update requires remove/insert operations
- **Top-K Query**: O(K log n) - traverse at most K score buckets
- **Space**: O(n) - store user-score mappings and score buckets

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of sliding-window quantile estimation with bounded memory—a practical problem in latency monitoring where old samples must fade out over time.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window maintenance using a circular buffer
- Fixed-bin histogram design and bucket mapping
- Error bounds derived from bucket width

## Bucket Mapping
The value range [0, M) is divided into S equal-width buckets. A value x maps to bucket floor(x * S / M), giving each bucket a width of M/S.

## Choosing S
The number of buckets S trades off memory (O(S)) and accuracy. With bucket width M/S, the P95 estimate has error at most M/(2S). For 1% error, choose S ≈ 50*M. In practice, S = 100-1000 works well for typical latency ranges.

## Error Guarantees vs Exact Computation
- **Approximate P95**: The estimated percentile falls within the bucket containing the true P95. Maximum error is half the bucket width: M/(2S).
- **Exact P95**: Requires maintaining all W samples in the window and sorting them, which is O(W log W) per query.
- **Trade-off**: This histogram approach gives O(1) updates and O(S) queries with bounded error, vs O(1) updates and O(W log W) queries for exact computation.

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingWindowP95 {
  constructor(M, W, S) {
    this.M = M;
    this.W = W;
    this.S = S;
    this.buffer = new Int32Array(W);
    this.hist = new Int32Array(S);
    this.head = 0;
    this.count = 0;
  }

  add(x) {
    const b = Math.floor((x * this.S) / this.M);
    this.hist[b]++;

    if (this.count === this.W) {
      const y = this.buffer[this.head];
      const by = Math.floor((y * this.S) / this.M);
      this.hist[by]--;
    } else {
      this.count++;
    }

    this.buffer[this.head] = x;
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.W;
  }

  query() {
    const p95 = Math.floor(0.95 * this.count);
    let cum = 0;
    for (let b = 0; b < this.S; b++) {
      cum += this.hist[b];
      if (cum >= p95) return (b * this.M) / this.S;
    }
    return this.M;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for non-uniform distributions or dynamic M?
- How does the choice of S affect query latency in a high-throughput system?

## Why This Is Asked
This checks understanding of streaming data structures and complexity trade-offs for maintaining order statistics under batch updates.

## Key Concepts
- Running median via two heaps
- Per-element insert cost and batch amortization
- Memory growth with total elements

## Code Example
```javascript
class MedianMaintainer {
  constructor() {
    this.low = new MaxHeap();
    this.high = new MinHeap();
  }
  add(x) {
    if (this.low.isEmpty() || x <= this.low.peek()) this.low.push(x);
    else this.high.push(x);
    // rebalance
    if (this.low.size() > this.high.size() + 1) this.high.push(this.low.pop());
    else if (this.high.size() > this.low.size()) this.low.push(this.high.pop());
  }
  median() {
    if (this.low.size() === this.high.size()) return (this.low.peek() + this.high.peek()) / 2;
    return this.low.peek();
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does varying batch size B affect latency and memory?
- How would you extend to handle sliding windows or duplicates efficiently?

